in the midst of what's considered the  nation's worst public health crisis so  far in the 21st century one Ohio County  is dramatically reducing the number of  deaths related to opioid overdoses first  on CBS this morning a new report shows  overdose deaths plunged by 31% in  Hamilton County which includes  Cincinnati there was also a 42 percent  drop in emergency room visits health  officials are crediting a new program  that gives away the overdose reversal  drug narcan for free well Don Dahler  went to Hamilton County to see how it  all works  Don good morning hopefully this can be a  model for others yes there is reason for  optimism good morning 115 Americans die  every day from opioid overdoses we went  to the epicenter of the national opioid  overdose epidemic and spoke to people  who are facing the crisis head-on it's  just heartbreaking what what's going on  out there and it's got to stop it's an  awful thing for a mom to go through  Kathy Meade watched her daughter Amy  struggle with opioid addiction from age  14 to 30 every time my phone would ring  I would think is this a coroner's office  calling for me to come and identify her  body she even used while pregnant with  her daughter Chloe I finally decided  that the right thing to do and the best  thing for her was for me to sign away my  parental rights  Chloe was one of more than  two-and-a-half million children living  with grandparents because of a parent's  addiction it makes me feel sad because  that she was early around for my wife  and while she missed out on important  milestones it's hard for me to look at  photos of her which she was young  because I wasn't there when she took her  first steps I wasn't there when she  started school this area that's been  consistently for the last several years  one of the hardest hit areas Ohio had  more than 4,800 accidental drug overdose  deaths last year 444 of those were in  Hamilton County Tim Ingram is the  Hamilton County Public Health Commission  how m